WEBVTT

00:00.450 --> 00:03.976
This is Part 6: fix problems and old systems.

00:03.926 --> 00:05.454
Look at the colour.

00:05.454 --> 00:07.317
It tells you what's wrong.

00:08.282 --> 00:10.963
Dim or yellow lights mean voltage drop.

00:10.913 --> 00:13.425
They're too far from the last injection.

00:13.425 --> 00:15.485
Add an injection closer.

00:15.485 --> 00:19.281
Check it's every fifty lights, and use sixteen gauge feeder.

00:19.281 --> 00:21.735
Solid blue means signal loss.

00:21.735 --> 00:24.281
No data is reaching those lights.

00:24.281 --> 00:26.943
Check every connector, plus to plus.

00:26.943 --> 00:29.489
The booster arrow must point to the lights.

00:29.489 --> 00:33.610
Change a bad booster, and change the first blue light if it's damaged.

00:33.610 --> 00:36.827
Solid white means a signal booster is missing or dead.

00:36.827 --> 00:38.934
Put one in, or change it.

00:38.934 --> 00:41.931
You need one on any extension over ten feet.

00:42.874 --> 00:46.064
Flicker means two power flows are meeting and fighting.

00:46.014 --> 00:48.665
Check the positive is cut at the injection.

00:48.665 --> 00:54.695
Move the injection further down the run, and add a booster just before the T. Wrong colours, or no response?

00:54.695 --> 00:56.651
The app setting is wrong.

00:56.651 --> 01:01.824
Set the IC type to Common RGBW, and the light count to the exact number put up.

01:01.824 --> 01:03.456
A breaker trips?

01:03.456 --> 01:06.304
Too many lights on that circuit, or a short.

01:06.304 --> 01:07.855
Count the lights.

01:07.855 --> 01:09.637
Over fifty-six?

01:09.637 --> 01:10.922
Split it.

01:10.922 --> 01:13.202
Look for a pinched or cut wire.

01:13.202 --> 01:15.308
Never put in a bigger breaker.

01:15.308 --> 01:17.276
The whole box shuts off?

01:17.276 --> 01:19.290
The supply is overloaded.

01:19.290 --> 01:22.415
Unplug it for five minutes, then do Test C.

01:22.415 --> 01:25.308
Over twenty amps on a six hundred watt box?

01:25.308 --> 01:28.352
Move lights to another box, and call your lead.

01:29.298 --> 01:31.470
Long runs lose volts.

01:31.420 --> 01:34.267
We tested fifty feet of twenty gauge wire.

01:34.267 --> 01:38.098
In at twenty-four point one volts, out at twenty-two point seven.

01:38.098 --> 01:42.010
That's a five point eight percent drop, past our five percent limit.

01:42.010 --> 01:46.350
Our fifty-light groups are only thirty-three feet, and that keeps the drop small.

01:46.350 --> 01:48.769
The fix is never a bigger supply.

01:48.769 --> 01:50.702
It's a closer injection.

01:51.650 --> 01:55.465
On a service call, first find out which system you're standing under.

01:55.415 --> 01:59.732
Mark 1: our first boxes, three fifty watts at twelve volts.

01:59.732 --> 02:02.672
One wire per injection, but no breakers.

02:02.672 --> 02:04.049
Don't touch it.

02:04.050 --> 02:06.411
Write the address and tell your lead.

02:06.411 --> 02:11.156
Mark 2: the old board, four fifty to six hundred watts at twenty-four volts.

02:11.156 --> 02:16.746
Often one fourteen-two feeding many injections, and only two five amp breakers in the whole box.

02:16.746 --> 02:18.124
Don't touch it.

02:18.124 --> 02:20.543
It's first in line for a retrofit.

02:20.543 --> 02:23.610
Mark 3: the new board with six breakers.

02:23.610 --> 02:28.066
One feeder per circuit, four amps each, positive cut at every injection.

02:28.066 --> 02:29.906
That's normal service.

02:29.906 --> 02:33.378
Stay inside fifty, max fifty-six lights.

02:33.378 --> 02:36.492
Never change a Mark 1 or Mark 2 in the field.

02:36.492 --> 02:38.656
Book it for a proper retrofit.

02:39.610 --> 02:45.115
Stop and call your lead if you see: lights wired straight into the supply, with no breaker or fuse.

02:45.065 --> 02:48.040
One wire feeding two or more injections.

02:48.040 --> 02:50.945
More than fifty-six lights between injections.

02:50.945 --> 02:52.993
Any twelve volt system.

02:52.993 --> 02:55.725
A breaker that trips when the client picks white.

02:55.725 --> 03:00.343
Wire through a wall with no ETL, CSA or UL number.

03:00.343 --> 03:04.359
Wire that looks brown, stiff or melted, or smells burnt.

03:04.359 --> 03:06.361
Or solid copper wire.

03:07.314 --> 03:10.099
Never add lights to a Mark 1 or Mark 2.

03:10.049 --> 03:11.704
They're already full.

03:11.704 --> 03:14.517
Never put in a bigger breaker to stop trips.

03:14.517 --> 03:17.538
And never leave an old run live without metering it.

03:17.538 --> 03:19.424
What do you tell the client?

03:19.424 --> 03:21.751
We moved to a new power system.

03:21.751 --> 03:24.632
It's more reliable and protects the circuit better.

03:24.632 --> 03:27.838
You're on our list for the upgrade, and there's no charge.

03:27.838 --> 03:30.153
Don't talk about fire or blame.

03:30.153 --> 03:32.850
If they keep asking, it goes to your lead.

03:33.818 --> 03:38.779
There are three ways to fix an old job, and each one starts with the power off at the breaker.

03:38.729 --> 03:43.463
A: a new board, when the old job already has one wire per injection.

03:43.463 --> 03:47.977
Each old wire goes on its own four amp circuit, and the controller on its own.

03:47.977 --> 03:51.252
Count the lights per group and split any over fifty-six.

03:51.252 --> 03:55.326
B: new wire, when one wire feeds many injections.

03:55.326 --> 04:01.183
Find every injection point, pull new sixteen-two to each one, and cut the positive at every injection.

04:01.183 --> 04:06.229
C: a small Class 2 driver at a plug near the roofline, mounted in an enclosure.

04:06.229 --> 04:07.861
Cut the positive.

04:07.861 --> 04:11.623
The driver feeds forward, and data still runs from the box.

04:11.623 --> 04:13.648
Then meter on Pure White.

04:13.648 --> 04:16.518
For A and B, do Test C too.

04:17.482 --> 04:18.716
Recap.

04:18.666 --> 04:21.027
Dim or yellow is voltage drop.

04:21.027 --> 04:23.412
Solid blue is signal loss.

04:23.412 --> 04:25.495
Solid white is the booster.

04:25.495 --> 04:27.960
Flicker means the positive isn't cut.

04:27.960 --> 04:30.680
Mark 1 and Mark 2: don't touch.

04:30.680 --> 04:32.659
And never a bigger breaker.

04:33.618 --> 04:35.315
That's Part 6.

04:35.265 --> 04:38.274
Now take the Part 6 quiz in your training portal.
